---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";

const c = site.company;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the company behind Custom Stickers Printing"
  description={`Label and sticker brand of ${c.legalNameZh} (${c.legalNameEn}), Yiwu, China. Registration number, D-U-N-S and how to verify us before you pay.`}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "About" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>About {site.brand}</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.08rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        {site.brand} is the label and sticker brand of {c.legalNameZh} ({c.legalNameEn}),
        a registered business in Yiwu, Zhejiang, China. We quote, specify and quality-check custom
        labels and stickers from {site.moq} units, and ship them worldwide.
      </p>

      <h2>Company details</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Trading name</th><td>{site.brand} — {site.domain}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Legal entity</th><td>{c.legalNameZh} · {c.legalNameEn}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Business type</th><td>{c.businessType}, registered in China</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Registration no.</th><td>{c.uscc} (统一社会信用代码)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">D-U-N-S number</th><td>{c.duns}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Registered location</th><td>{c.city}, {c.region}, {c.country}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Principal</th><td>{c.founder}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Contact</th><td><a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> · <a href={`https://wa.me/${site.whatsapp}`} rel="noopener">{site.whatsappDisplay}</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How to verify us before you pay</h2>
      <p>
        A business that asks for a bank transfer should be checkable. Three ways, in order of speed:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Company registry.</strong> Search {c.uscc} in China's National Enterprise Credit
          Information Publicity System (<a href="https://www.gsxt.gov.cn/index.html" rel="noopener">gsxt.gov.cn</a>).
          The record shows the registered name, location and status.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>D-U-N-S.</strong> {c.duns} can be looked up through Dun &amp; Bradstreet
          (<a href="https://www.dnb.com/duns-number/lookup.html" rel="noopener">dnb.com</a>), which is the number
          used in international trade and by some payment providers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The work itself.</strong> Ask for a physical sample on your own container before a full run, or a
          short video call to see the labels being checked. We offer both, and neither requires an order.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we actually do</h2>
      <p>
        The work is specification, pre-press and quality control, not running a press. We take your artwork and
        container, decide which material and adhesive will survive them, check the file for bleed, colour and cut
        paths, place the job with a printing partner in China, inspect the output against the approved proof, and
        arrange the shipment.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the honest division of labour: production capacity sits with the presses, and the judgement about
        what to print on what sits here. It is also why we can run {site.moq} units when a converter with its own
        plant will not — we are not protecting a press schedule.
      </p>

      <h2>What we print</h2>
      <p>
        Product and packaging labels, bottle and jar labels, cosmetic and food labels, waterproof and clear film
        labels, roll labels for applicators, vinyl and die-cut stickers, logo stickers and sticker sheets. Every
        order starts at {site.moq} units per design and is quoted per project, because a 30 mm circle and a
        90 × 120 mm wrap do not share a unit price.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not claim</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>That we operate a factory. We do not, and saying so would be the first thing a buyer checks.</li>
        <li>A certification, test result or factory audit we cannot produce on request.</li>
        <li>A price before we know the size, material and quantity — those three decide it.</li>
        <li>A delivery date that ignores the approval step, which is where most schedules actually slip.</li>
        <li>Customer names or logos without written permission, which is why you will not see a wall of them here.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Quality control</h2>
      <p>
        Every job is checked against the approved proof before packing: registration, colour against the approved
        reference, die-cut alignment, and adhesion on a production sample. If something falls outside tolerance we
        tell you before it ships, not after the parcel arrives. Where a problem is ours, we reprint; where it comes
        from artwork approved as supplied, we will say so plainly and quote the fix.
      </p>

      <h2>Payment and invoicing</h2>
      <p>
        Quotations and invoices are issued in the name of {c.legalNameEn}. Payment is by bank transfer to the
        account shown on the invoice, or by an agreed platform method; we do not take card details over chat or
        email. The full banking details — account name, number and SWIFT — appear only on the invoice issued for a
        confirmed order, never on this website or in an unsolicited message.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        If you receive payment instructions that differ from your invoice, stop and confirm with us at
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> before sending anything. Invoice fraud is common in
        this trade and a five-minute check prevents it.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we work with</h2>
      <p>
        Skincare and candle brands launching a first SKU, food and beverage producers testing a new container,
        ecommerce sellers who need packaging stickers in short runs, and agencies managing several client brands at
        once. The common thread is a quantity too small for a traditional label converter and too specific for a
        consumer sticker site.
      </p>

      <h2>About the knowledge base</h2>
      <p>
        The technical articles on this site are written by the same team that quotes the work, from the problems
        that arrive in quotations. Where a claim rests on a published standard — adhesion testing, colour control,
        proofing, barcode grading — the standard is linked at the foot of the article. Where no standard applies,
        we say what the recommendation is based on rather than attaching an unrelated reference.
      </p>

      <p class="small">
        Last reviewed: November 2026. Corrections and questions about this page:
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
